# Line endings, decided once for the whole repository.
#
# Git for Windows ships with core.autocrlf=true, so a plain `git clone` there
# rewrites every text file's LF into CRLF on the way to the working tree. That
# is invisible until something reads a repository file AS TEXT and compares it,
# and this suite does that in about ninety places: README.md's section order,
# `<main\n\s+id="canvas"` in App.tsx, the class attributes swept out of every
# .tsx. A `\n` in one of those patterns simply stops matching once a `\r` is
# sitting in front of it, and the failure names the assertion rather than the
# cause — twenty tests reporting twenty unrelated-looking things, all of them
# the same byte.
#
# `eol=lf` is the fix at the layer the problem lives on. The alternative was a
# `.replace(/\r/g, "")` in every reader, which is the same fix written eighty
# times and forgotten by the eighty-first: nothing reminds the next person who
# adds a readFileSync that the string they just got may not be the bytes in the
# commit. Pinning the checkout means every platform reads what was committed.
#
# `text=auto` leaves Git's own binary detection in charge of WHICH files this
# applies to, so a file it decides is binary is copied through untouched; the
# PNG below is called out anyway, because a hero screenshot silently corrupted
# by an EOL conversion is not a failure any test here would report.
#
# One thing `text=auto` will NOT do, and it is the usual reason this rule looks
# like it did nothing: a file ALREADY IN GIT WITH CRLF is exempt from
# conversion on both checkin and checkout, `eol` included. Adding this file
# fixes nothing for such content — `git add --renormalize .` does, once, from a
# clean tree. There is none here: `git ls-files --eol` reports `i/lf` for all
# 323 tracked files and `-text` for the screenshot, so this rule takes effect
# on the next checkout everywhere and rewrites nothing that is committed.
* text=auto eol=lf

*.png binary

# The exceptions, ahead of anyone adding one. cmd.exe genuinely wants CRLF: a
# batch file with LF endings can exit silently, and `goto`/`call` label
# resolution breaks outright, because the scan for a label's end is defined
# around \r\n. PowerShell tolerates LF and is conventionally CRLF. The repo has
# none of these today, and the day it grows one is exactly the day nobody will
# remember that the rule above applies to it too.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
